--TEST--
§ 14's standard streams are `Core\IO::stdin` alone — there is no `stdout` half, because writing to that stream is `Core\Cli::write`'s sink — spec § 14, ADR 0086 §§ 1 and 7
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The reading half is here, and it is a value rather than a handle: a
// descriptor the process was handed has no position to seek and no length to
// truncate, and a `close` on it would take the stream from the whole process.
tainted string $input = Core\IO::stdin();
echo "read=", Core\Str::length($input), "\n";

// The writing half is not here at all. ADR 0086 § 1's substitution is uniform
// -- not tty-dependent, not qualifier-dependent -- so a raw descriptor onto
// standard output would be the way around the sink rather than a second
// convenience beside it, and `Core\Cli::write` already is that operation.
Core\IO::stdout();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\IO` has no member named `stdout`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
